22:23:37 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I love that analogy, because it actually proves the opposite of what you intend—and it does so perfectly. Let’s run with it. Imagine you have a stove at home. You can toss in some dry, clean firewood, or you can shovel in some wet, creosote-laden coal. In the short term, both “make heat in the end,” sure. But after a few days of burning the wrong fuel: - The chimney gets clogged with tar and soot, reducing the draft—your stove becomes less efficient at burning anything. (Metabolic slowdown.) - Your room fills with smoke and toxic gases, making you feel lousy and short of breath. (Inflammation, oxidative stress.) - The internal parts corrode faster because the fuel burned dirty, leaving acidic residues. (Tissue damage, plaque buildup in arteries.) - You have to keep feeding it constantly because the low-quality fuel doesn’t sustain a steady burn. (Blood sugar crashes, cravings.) Eventually, you burn out the stove entirely, even though you supplied the same “calories” of heat energy on paper. The human body isn’t a lab-calibrated bomb calorimeter that just combusts things to carbon dioxide and water. It’s a *stove that has to live in its own exhaust*, build and repair its own parts from the raw materials you give it, and juggle complex chemistry to stay functional for decades. Food isn’t just “burned for energy”—the amino acids become your muscle, the fatty acids become your cell membranes and brain tissue, the fiber feeds your gut bacteria which then produce compounds that protect your intestines. None of that shows up if you only measure heat output. So to tweak the analogy: “You care what you burn because the stove isn’t just a firebox, it’s also the house, the chimney, and the person breathing the air. Feed it waste and it’ll fall apart, even if the thermometer says it’s warm today.” The simplicity of “just heat” is tempting, but it only works if you ignore that a living body is a self-maintaining system, not a furnace. That’s why the quality of the fuel matters immensely—just as any mechanic would tell you about an engine.
