lons are 10 gallons. But if you pour diesel into the Ferrari, the “energy in” number on the pump was correct, but the outcome is a wrecked engine. The fuel gauge wasn’t misleading—it was perfectly accurate about volume—but the *engine’s design* demands a specific type of fuel to actually turn that chemical energy into forward motion without destroying itself. Our bodies are more flexible than a Ferrari engine, but the principle stands. The label doesn’t capture the “other stuff” because that’s not its job. It’s not fundamentally misleading; it’s just one tool. You, the person eating, need both the tool (energy count) and the owner’s manual (what kinds of fuel keep the machine healthy). So to your binary: **the basic “it’s all just energy” idea is right in a physics sense, but incomplete in a biology sense. That incompleteness doesn’t make calorie labels or health class fraudulent—it just means they’re the first chapter, not the whole book.**
