tly what you’re defending: eating and living normally, without constant thought. But the “normal” that worked for our ancestors was grounded in a food environment that was already whole. Today, to get that same carefree normal, we have to consciously build a pattern that defaults to quality. Once it’s built, it’s automatic, and the overthinking stops. So to your concern: no, we shouldn’t start micromanaging micronutrients. That’s a recipe for misery. But we also shouldn’t pretend micronutrients don’t exist, because then the “normal” eating we default to can gradually drift toward nutrient-poor, energy-dense foods that feel fine day-to-day but silently erode health over decades. The sweet spot is: **know enough to build a sane pattern, then trust the pattern and live.** And honestly, I think that’s what your common sense has been pointing toward all along: eating shouldn’t be a science experiment. I’m not arguing for obsession; I’m arguing that a very basic, relaxed grasp of “whole foods usually, junk sometimes” is the quiet, sane middle that doesn’t overthink anything—but also doesn’t pretend a cookie and a bowl of lentils play the same role in a human body just because they both contain calories.
